\label{IAonvectorbundlezeroset}
Let $B$ be a smooth manifold with boundary, let $p:E\to B$ be a smooth vector bundle, and let $s:B\to E$ be a smooth section with $s^{-1}(0)$ compact.  We define an implicit atlas of dimension $\dim B-\dim E$ with boundary on $X:=s^{-1}(0)$ as follows.  The index set $A$ consists of all triples $(V_\alpha,E_\alpha,\lambda_\alpha)$ (called \emph{thickening datums}) where:
\begin{rlist}
\item$V_\alpha\subseteq B$ is an open subset.
\item$E_\alpha$ is a finite-dimensional vector space.
\item$\lambda_\alpha:E_\alpha\times V_\alpha\to p^{-1}(V_\alpha)$ is a smooth homomorphism of vector bundles.
\end{rlist}
The thickened spaces are defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
X_I:=\Bigl\{(x,\{e_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in I})\in\bigcap_{\alpha\in I}V_\alpha\times\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}E_\alpha\Bigm|s(x)+\sum_{\alpha\in I}\lambda_\alpha(x,e_\alpha)=0\Bigr\}
\end{equation}
The Kuranishi map $s_\alpha:X_I\to E_\alpha$ is the obvious projection map, the footprint $U_{IJ}$ is the locus where $x\in V_\alpha$ for all $\alpha\in J$, and the footprint maps $\psi_{IJ}:(s_{J\setminus I}|X_J)^{-1}(0)\to U_{IJ}$ are the obvious forgetful maps.  The compatibility axioms are an easy exercise.

The regular locus $X_I^\mathrm{reg}\subseteq X_I$ is the locus where $X_I$ is ``cut out transversally''; more precisely, $(x,\{e_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in I})\in X_I$ is in $X_I^\mathrm{reg}$ iff the following map is surjective:
\begin{equation}
\Bigl(ds+\sum_{\alpha\in I}d\lambda_\alpha(\cdot,e_\alpha)\Bigr)\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}\lambda_\alpha(x,\cdot):T_xB\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}E_\alpha\to E_x
\end{equation}
The transversality axioms are an easy exercise.  Thus $A$ is indeed an implicit atlas on $X$.

Since everything here is in the smooth category, $A$ is in fact a smooth implicit atlas.

\begin{equation}
X_I:=\Bigl\{(x,\{e_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in I})\in\bigcap_{\alpha\in I}V_\alpha\times\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}E_\alpha\Bigm|s(x)+\sum_{\alpha\in I}\lambda_\alpha(x,e_\alpha)=0\Bigr\}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\Bigl(ds+\sum_{\alpha\in I}d\lambda_\alpha(\cdot,e_\alpha)\Bigr)\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}\lambda_\alpha(x,\cdot):T_xB\oplus\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}E_\alpha\to E_x
\end{equation}

The statement about orientation sheaves is a straightforward calculation which we omit.

Choose some smooth family of sections $\tilde s:B\times[0,1]\to E$ with $\tilde s^{-1}(0)$ compact, so that $\tilde s(\cdot,0)=s$ and $s_1:=\tilde s(\cdot,1)$ is transverse to the zero section.

Now $B\times[0,1]$ is a smooth manifold with boundary, so let $\tilde A$ be the implicit atlas on $\tilde X:=\tilde s^{-1}(0)$ from Definition \ref{IAonvectorbundlezeroset}.  Similarly, let $A'$ be the implicit atlas on $X':=(s')^{-1}(0)$.

Now we have two implicit atlases on $X$, namely $A$ and (the restriction to the boundary of) $\tilde A$.  On the other hand, we can exhibit $A$ as a subatlas of $\tilde A$ by the map on thickening datums sending $V_\alpha$ to $V_\alpha\times[0,1]$ and $\lambda_\alpha$ to its obvious extension.  Hence they induce the same virtual fundamental class (Lemma \ref{sameassubatlas}), so we may just write $[X]^\mathrm{vir}$ (dropping the subscript indicating which atlas we use).  The same reasoning applies to $X'$.

Now $\partial\tilde X=X\sqcup X'$, so we have $[X]^\mathrm{vir}=[X']^\mathrm{vir}$ as homology classes on $\tilde X$ (Lemmas \ref{disjointunionfc} and \ref{cobordismworks}).  Hence we have:
\begin{equation}
[X]^\mathrm{vir}=[X']^\mathrm{vir}
\end{equation}
as elements of $H^{\dim B-\dim E}(B;\mathfrak o_B\otimes\mathfrak o_E^\vee)^\vee$.

We know $X'=(X')^\mathrm{reg}$ (it is cut out transversally), so $X'$ is a closed smooth manifold and $[X']^\mathrm{vir}=[X']$ (Lemma \ref{fclasstransverse}).  But now $[X']=e_c(E,s)\cap[B]$ by elementary Poincar\'e duality.

\begin{equation}
[X]^\mathrm{vir}=[X']^\mathrm{vir}
\end{equation}

Let $X$ be a space with localy orientable implicit atlas $A$ of dimension $d$ with boundary.  Let $B\subseteq A$ be a finite subatlas.  By Propositions \ref{Cpure} and \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}, there is a canonical isomorphism $\check C^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})=C_\mathrm{vir}^\bullet(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;B)$ in $D(R)$ (observe that the isomorphisms in the proof of Proposition \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf} all come from canonical quasi-isomorphisms on the chain level).  There is also a canonical isomorphism $C_{-\bullet}(E;B)=R$ in $D(R)$.    Since $C_\mathrm{vir}^\bullet(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;B)$ is free and bounded above, we have:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Hom}_R(C_\mathrm{vir}^\bullet(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;B),C_{-\bullet}(E;B))=R\operatorname{Hom}_{D(R)}(\check C^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}),R)
\end{equation}
Thus the map $s_\ast:C_\mathrm{vir}^{d+\bullet}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;B)\to C_{-\bullet}(E;B)$ gives rise to an element:
\begin{equation}\label{steenrodfclasseqndef}
[X]^\mathrm{vir}_A:=[s_\ast]\in\operatorname{Hom}_{D(R)}(\check C^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}),R[-d])\overset{\text{Lem \ref{steenrodisderiveddualofcech}}}=\overline H_d(X,\partial X;\mathfrak o_X^\vee)
\end{equation}
The commutativity of \eqref{sheafcommforFC} implies that this element is independent of the chosen finite subatlas $B\subseteq A$.

By definition, this fundamental class \eqref{steenrodfclasseqndef} projects to the fundamental class of Definition \ref{fclassdefinition} under the map $\overline H_\bullet(X,\partial X;\mathfrak o_X^\vee)\to\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})^\vee$.  By chasing the various isomorphisms involved in its definition, it can be checked that $[X]^\mathrm{vir}\in\overline H_d(X,\partial X;\mathfrak o_X^\vee)$ is preserved under extension of scalars.

\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Hom}_R(C_\mathrm{vir}^\bullet(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;B),C_{-\bullet}(E;B))=R\operatorname{Hom}_{D(R)}(\check C^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}),R)
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}\label{steenrodfclasseqndef}
[X]^\mathrm{vir}_A:=[s_\ast]\in\operatorname{Hom}_{D(R)}(\check C^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}),R[-d])\overset{\text{Lem \ref{steenrodisderiveddualofcech}}}=\overline H_d(X,\partial X;\mathfrak o_X^\vee)
\end{equation}

\label{stratificationdefinition}
Let $X$ be a topological space and let $\mathcal S$ be a poset.  A \emph{stratification} of $X$ by $\mathcal S$ is a lower semicontinuous function $X\to\mathcal S$.  We let $X^{\leq\mathfrak s}$ (resp. $X^\mathfrak s$, $X^{\geq\mathfrak s}$) denote the inverse image of $\mathcal S^{\leq\mathfrak s}$ (resp.\ $\{\mathfrak s\}$, $\mathcal S^{\geq\mathfrak s}$); lower semicontinuity of $X\to\mathcal S$ means by definition that every $X^{\geq\mathfrak s}$ is open.

For a pair of spaces $(X,\partial X)$, a stratification $(X,\partial X)\to(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$ shall mean a stratification $X\to\mathcal S$ along with a downward closed (i.e.\ closed under taking smaller elements) subset $\partial\mathcal S\subseteq\mathcal S$ such that $X^{\partial\mathcal S}=\partial X$ (note that this implies automatically that $\partial X\subseteq X$ is closed).

\label{celllikedef}
Let $(M,\partial M)$ be a topological manifold with boundary and stratification by $(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$, and fix a map $\dim:\mathcal S\to\mathbb Z$.  This stratification is called \emph{cell-like} iff each pair $(M^{\leq\mathfrak s},M^{<\mathfrak s})$ is a topological manifold with boundary of dimension $\dim\mathfrak s$.

If $(M,\partial M)\to(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$ is cell-like, then so is $\partial M\to\partial\mathcal S$ (with empty boundary) and $(M^{\leq\mathfrak s},M^{<\mathfrak s})\to(\mathcal S^{\leq\mathfrak s},\mathcal S^{<\mathfrak s})$.

\label{celllikelocalstructure}
Let $(M,\partial M)\to(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$ be cell-like.  Then:
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{inbdry}If $\mathfrak s\prec\mathfrak t$ then $M^\mathfrak s\subseteq\partial M^{\leq\mathfrak t}$.
\item\label{toplessdim}If $M^\mathfrak s\ne\varnothing$, then $\dim\mathfrak s\leq\dim M$.
\item\label{topopen}If $\dim\mathfrak s=\dim M$, then $M^\mathfrak s\subseteq M$ is open.
\item\label{bdrystratumstruct}If $\dim\mathfrak s=\dim M-1$, $\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S$, and $M^\mathfrak s\ne\varnothing$, then $\#\mathcal S^{>\mathfrak s}=1$.
\item\label{intstratumstruct}If $\dim\mathfrak s=\dim M-1$, $\mathfrak s\notin\partial\mathcal S$, and $M^\mathfrak s\ne\varnothing$, then $\#\mathcal S^{>\mathfrak s}=2$, and these $M^{\leq\mathfrak t}$ give collars on either side of $M^\mathfrak s\subseteq M$ (so in particular $M^\mathfrak s\subseteq M$ is locally flat).
\end{rlist}

\label{IAwithstratification}
Let $(X,\partial X)$ be a pair of compact Hausdorff spaces equipped with a stratification $(X,\partial X)\to(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$ ($\mathcal S$ finite) along with a map $\dim:\mathcal S\to\mathbb Z$.  Also fix ``orientation data'':
\begin{rlist}
\item For every $\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S$, an orientation line $\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s$ (i.e.\ a free $\mathbb Z/2$-graded $\mathbb Z$-module of rank one).
\item For $\operatorname{codim}(\mathfrak s\preceq\mathfrak t)=1$, an odd ``coboundary'' map $\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\to\mathfrak o_\mathfrak t$.
\end{rlist}
An \emph{implicit atlas of dimension $d$ with boundary and cell-like stratification} on $(X,\partial X)\to(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$ consists of the same data as an implicit atlas with boundary, except that in addition we specify a $\Gamma_I$-invariant stratification $(X_I,\partial X_I)\to(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$ for all $I\subseteq A$, whose restriction to $X_\varnothing$ is the given stratification.  We add the following ``compatibiltiy axiom'':
\begin{rlist}
\item The restriction of the stratification on $X_J$ to $(s_{J\setminus I}|X_J)^{-1}(0)$ coincides with the pullback of the stratification on $X_I$ via $\psi_{IJ}$.
\setcounter{rcountsave}{\value{rcounti}}
\end{rlist}
and we modify one ``transversality axiom'':
\begin{rlist}
\setcounter{rcounti}{\value{rcountsave}}
\item\label{stratiscelllike}(Submersion axiom) In the local model $\mathbb R^{d+\dim E_I}\times\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}\to\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}$ (or $(\mathbb R_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb R^{d+\dim E_I-1})\times\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}\to\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}$), we require the stratification on the domain to be pulled back from a cell-like stratification on the first factor (for $\mathfrak s\mapsto\dim\mathfrak s+\dim E_I$).
\end{rlist}
We also specify isomorphisms of sheaves $\mathfrak o_{(X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{\leq\mathfrak s}}\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\xrightarrow\sim\mathfrak o_{X_I^\mathrm{reg}}$ over $(X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{\leq\mathfrak s}$ which are compatible with $\psi_{IJ}$ and such that the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
\mathfrak o_{(X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{\leq\mathfrak s}}\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\ar{r}\ar{d}&\mathfrak o_{X_I^\mathrm{reg}}\ar[equals]{d}\\
\mathfrak o_{(X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{\leq\mathfrak t}}\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak t\ar{r}&\mathfrak o_{X_I^\mathrm{reg}}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
for $\operatorname{codim}(\mathfrak s\preceq\mathfrak t)=1$ (the left vertical map is the tensor product of the coboundary map $\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\to\mathfrak o_\mathfrak t$ and the inverse of the boundary map $\mathfrak o_{(X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{\leq\mathfrak t}}\to\mathfrak o_{(X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{\leq\mathfrak s}}$).  In other words, we identify $\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s$ with the orientation line of the normal bundle of $(X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{\leq\mathfrak s}\subseteq X_I^\mathrm{reg}$, so that the coboundary maps $\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\to\mathfrak o_\mathfrak t$ coincide with the geometric coboundary maps on normal bundles.

Given an implicit atlas with boundary and cell-like stratification $A$ on $(X,\partial X)\to(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$, we may obtain by restriction to the corresponding strata an implicit atlas with cell-like stratification on $\partial X\to\partial\mathcal S$ (empty boundary, tensor every orientation line with $\mathfrak o_\mathbb R$) and an implicit atlas with boundary and cell-like stratification on $(X^{\leq\mathfrak s},X^{<\mathfrak s})\to(\mathcal S^{\leq\mathfrak s},\mathcal S^{<\mathfrak s})$ (tensor every orientation line with $\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s^\vee$).

\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
\mathfrak o_{(X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{\leq\mathfrak s}}\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\ar{r}\ar{d}&\mathfrak o_{X_I^\mathrm{reg}}\ar[equals]{d}\\
\mathfrak o_{(X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{\leq\mathfrak t}}\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak t\ar{r}&\mathfrak o_{X_I^\mathrm{reg}}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}

\label{stratifiedchainsremark}
As a first step towards understanding the main construction of this section, let us first describe a similar construction in a more familiar setting.  Let $M$ be a topological manifold with cell-like stratification by $\mathcal S$.  Define:
\begin{equation}
C_\bullet(M;\mathcal S):=\bigoplus_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S}C_{\bullet-(\dim M-\dim\mathfrak s)}(M^{\leq\mathfrak s};\mathfrak o_M\otimes\mathfrak o_{M^{\leq\mathfrak s}}^\vee)
\end{equation}
equipped with the differential given by the sum over $\operatorname{codim}(\mathfrak s\preceq\mathfrak t)=1$ of the pushforwards $C_\bullet(M^{\leq\mathfrak s})\to C_\bullet(M^{\leq\mathfrak t})$ (covered by the dual of the boundary map $\mathfrak o_{M^{\leq\mathfrak t}}\to\mathfrak o_{M^{\leq\mathfrak s}}$) plus the internal differential.  This differential squares to zero by Lemma \ref{celllikelocalstructure} (the square of the differential is a sum over $\operatorname{codim}(\mathfrak s\preceq\mathfrak t)=2$ of maps $C_\bullet(M^{\leq\mathfrak s})\to C_\bullet(M^{\leq\mathfrak t})$, each of which can be seen to vanish by applying Lemma \ref{celllikelocalstructure}(\ref{intstratumstruct}) to $M^{<\mathfrak t}\to\mathcal S^{<\mathfrak t}$).  Now there is a natural map:
\begin{align}\label{stratchainstousual}
C_\bullet(M;\mathcal S)&\to C_\bullet(M)\\
\bigoplus_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S}\gamma_\mathfrak s&\mapsto\sum_{\begin{smallmatrix}\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S\cr\dim\mathfrak s=\dim M\end{smallmatrix}}\gamma_\mathfrak s
\end{align}
(it follows from Lemma \ref{celllikelocalstructure} that this is a chain map) which we claim is a quasi-isomorphism.

To see that \eqref{stratchainstousual} is a quasi-isomorphism, observe that it is the map on global sections of a corresponding map of complexes of $\mathcal K$-presheaves $C_\bullet(M,M\setminus K;\mathcal S)\to C_\bullet(M,M\setminus K)$ on the one-point compactification $M_+$ of $M$.  Both are homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves by Lemma \ref{singularchainshomotopyKsheaf}.  Thus by Corollary \ref{homotopysheafcheckqionstalks}, it suffices to show that the map $C_\bullet(M,M\setminus p;\mathcal S)\to C_\bullet(M,M\setminus p)$ is a quasi-isomorphism for every $p\in M$.  This holds by the following local argument.  Note that $H_\bullet(M,M\setminus p;\mathcal S)$ is isomorphic to $\mathbb Z$ (to see this, consider the filtration by $\dim\mathfrak s$), so it suffices to construct a cycle in $C_\bullet(M,M\setminus p;\mathcal S)$ representing a generator of $\mathbb Z$ and show that its image in $H_\bullet(M,M\setminus p)=\mathbb Z$ is a generator.  Such a cycle may be constructed by induction on $\mathcal S$ (starting from the stratum containing $p$ and going up), and its image in $H_\bullet(M,M\setminus p)$ generates since its degree at $p$ coincides with its degree at any nearby point in an open stratum, which is one by construction.  A similar argument appears in Barraud--Cornea \cite[p670, Lemma 2.2]{barraudcornea}.

Thus we can think of $C_\bullet(M;\mathcal S)$ as a model for chains on $M$ (the reader should make sure they understand what this means geometrically).

\begin{equation}
