An \emph{MF-set} is a set $\mathcal Y$ along with:
\begin{rlist}
\item For every $i\in\mathcal Y$, a PL manifold with cells $(M_i,\mathcal S_i)$.
\item(Face identifications). For every $i\in\mathcal Y$ and every $\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_i$, an index $j\in\mathcal Y$ and an isomorphism $(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s})\xrightarrow\sim(M_j,\mathcal S_j)$.  These indices and isomorphisms must be (strictly) transitive in the following obvious sense.  If $\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S$ is maximal, then $j=i$ and $(M_i,\mathcal S_i)=(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s})\xrightarrow\sim(M_j,\mathcal S_j)=(M_i,\mathcal S_i)$ is the identity map.  If $\mathfrak s'\preceq\mathfrak s$ is a nested pair of faces with identifications $(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s})\xrightarrow\sim(M_j,\mathcal S_j)$ and $(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s'},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s'})\xrightarrow\sim(M_{j'},\mathcal S_{j'})$, and the $\mathfrak s'$ face of $(M_j,\mathcal S_j)$ is identified $(M_j^{\leq\mathfrak s'},\mathcal S_j^{\leq\mathfrak s'})\xrightarrow\sim(M_k,\mathcal S_k)$, then $k=j'$ and the two identifications of $(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s'},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s'})$ with $(M_k,\mathcal S_k)=(M_{j'},\mathcal S_{j'})$ are the same.
\end{rlist}
A morphism of MF-sets $f:\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Y'$ is a map of sets covered by isomorphisms $(M_i,\mathcal S_i)\to(M_{f(i)},\mathcal S_{f(i)})$, compatible with the face identifications for $\mathcal Y$ and $\mathcal Y'$ in the obvious way.

The category of MF-sets has a natural symmetric monoidal structure: given MF-sets $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ and $\{M_j'\}_{j\in\mathcal Y'}$, their product is defined to be $\{M_i\times M_j'\}_{(i,j)\in\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}$, which is again an MF-set.

For an MF-set $\mathcal Y$, let $C^\mathcal Y_\bullet(X)$ denote the complex freely generated\footnote{A given map $M_i\to X$ contributes a copy of the orientation module of $M_i$, which is isomorphic to $\mathbb Z$ but not canonically so.} by maps $M_i\to X$ (for $i\in\mathcal Y$), with differential given by the obvious sum over all codimension one faces.  Note that there is a natural map:
\begin{equation}
C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)\otimes\mathcal C_\bullet^{\mathcal Y'}(X')\to C_\bullet^{\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}(X\times X')
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)\otimes\mathcal C_\bullet^{\mathcal Y'}(X')\to C_\bullet^{\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}(X\times X')
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}\label{homologyYcomparison}
H_\bullet(X)\to H_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)
\end{equation}

\begin{align}
M_i^\mathfrak s\times\{0\}&\quad\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_i\\
M_i^\mathfrak s\times(0,1)&\quad\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_i\\
M_i^\mathfrak t\times\{1\}&\quad\mathfrak t\in\mathcal F(T_i)
\end{align}

\label{SSMFdef}
An \emph{$S^1$-MF-set} is an MF-set $\mathcal Y$ along with a principal $S^1$-bundle $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$ where $(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$ (and hence also $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y$) is an increasing union of compact polyhedra (we do not require $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y$ to be contractible), and for every $i\in\mathcal Y$, a pullback diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{SSMFstructuremaps}
\begin{tikzcd}
M_i\ar{r}\ar{d}&(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\ar{d}\\
N_i\ar{r}&(BS^1)_\mathcal Y
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
with PL maps where $N_i$ (and hence also $M_i$) is a PL manifold, and where the stratification on $M_i$ is pulled back from $N_i$, such that these diagrams are compatible with the face identifications.  A map of $S^1$-MF-sets $\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Y'$ is said to be injective iff it is injective as a map of sets and the map $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(ES^1)_{\mathcal Y'}$ is injective.

There is a forgetful functor from $S^1$-MF-sets to MF-sets, where we remember $M_i$ (of course, there is another natural forgetful functor remembering $N_i$, though we will never use it).  When speaking of a morphism $\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Y'$ where $\mathcal Y$ is an $S^1$-MF-set and $\mathcal Y'$ is an MF-set, we implicitly apply the forgetful functor to $\mathcal Y$.

The category of $S^1$-MF-sets has a natural symmetric monoidal structure: given $S^1$-MF-sets $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ and $\{M_j'\}_{j\in\mathcal Y'}$, we may define their product $\{M_i\times M_j'\}_{(i,j)\in\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}$, which is again an $S^1$-MF-set, via the diagonal $S^1$-action on $M_i\times M_j'$ and $(ES^1)_{\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}:=(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\times(ES^1)_{\mathcal Y'}$ with the diagonal action.  The forgetful functor from $S^1$-MF-sets to MF-sets is clearly a symmetric monoidal functor.  It also makes sense to take the product of an $S^1$-MF-set and an MF-set.

For an $S^1$-MF-set $\mathcal Y$, let $C^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)$ (for $X$ an $S^1$-space) denote the complex generated by commuting diagrams of $S^1$-equivariant maps:
\begin{equation}\label{SSchaindiagram}
\begin{tikzcd}
